WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:03.879
The location, New York City, is established
during the opening title sequence, where we

00:00:03.879 --> 00:00:08.640
see huge text looming over skyscrapers in
Manhattan, eventually zeroing in on Times

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:11.269
Square, the home of many world class Broadway
shows.

00:00:11.269 --> 00:00:14.980
These theatrical performances don’t have
the luxury of jumping around from location

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:19.189
to location, and can oftentimes be set in
a single place, relying entirely on strong

00:00:19.189 --> 00:00:21.769
performances from each character to carry
the show.

00:00:21.769 --> 00:00:25.359
The same can be said of David Fincher’s
2002 film Panic Room.

00:00:25.359 --> 00:00:29.149
Outside of a short intro and outro scene,
the entirety of the movie takes place inside

00:00:29.149 --> 00:00:33.760
this house, which fittingly, is a giant set
where the script was shot in chronological

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:34.260
order.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:38.890
“The nice thing about our movie was that we actually
shot the entire film in sequence…

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:42.329
Everything happened exactly as it happens
in the film, we got to shoot for beginning

00:00:42.329 --> 00:00:42.820
to end.”

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:46.960
If these shots are a hint to the audience
to buckle in for a Broadway-like horror-suspense-thriller,

00:00:46.969 --> 00:00:50.710
the electronic billboard gives us a clue as
to what the main character is going to go

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:51.200
through.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.920
It flashes out a message: face your fears
-- something that our claustrophobic hero,

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:58.241
Meg Altman will have to come to terms with
after she and her daughter are forced to take

00:00:58.241 --> 00:01:00.440
cover in the tiny confines of the Panic Room.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.560
“Open it please.”

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:06.700
“All the kids he’s apparently got, no
wonder he wanted a place to hide.”

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:08.300
“Please open the door.”

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:09.640
“That is highly inappropriate.”

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:11.550
“Open the door.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:12.140
Please.”

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:16.580
But the fear of small spaces wouldn’t be
the only obstacle that Meg would have to overcome.

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:20.550
To learn the connection to David Fincher’s
sophomore film Se-seven-en and the truth about

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:24.540
how Ms. Altman alerted the police without
her daughter’s captors finding out, stick

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.420
around to the end of this video.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:32.320
Welcome to Things You Missed.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:36.710
In today’s episode I’ll be covering the
apparently under-appreciated Panic Room.

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:39.920
The only thing I don’t really get about
panic rooms, is why they are called panic

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:40.420
rooms.

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:42.680
I mean they are supposed to be the safest place in
the house, right?

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:45.140
So they should really call them “don’t
panic rooms.”

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:46.790
Or “the stay calm room.”

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:48.170
“STAY ****ing CALM!”

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:49.160
NOBODY PANIC!

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:52.700
I’m gonna roll the logo….

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:00.880
Early on in the film, we are given a tour
of the property.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.720
“Stop calling it ‘the property.’ It sounds
ridiculous.”

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:05.980
Actually, what may look like a tour of the…

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:08.100
house… is really

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:10.170
setting up the backstory of this movie.

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:13.520
We are given snippets of information about
the previous owner, Sidney Pearlstien.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:16.770
One line in particular tells us most of what
we need to know.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:20.660
“His kids are suing each other over the
estate.

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:22.280
He was a recluse.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:22.780
Rich.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:23.580
Paranoid.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:25.450
Now it turns out they can’t find half his
money.”

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:28.980
We later find out that one of those kids is
the burglar, Junior.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:30.580
Also known as Cornrow Leto.

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:36.660
Another important detail explains why Meg
is uncomfortable with tight spaces.

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:40.300
“This whole thing makes me nervous.”

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:41.600
“Why?”

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:43.340
“Ever read any Poe?”

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.220
“No, but I loved her last album.”

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:47.300
Update in 2020.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:48.980
Haunted is STILL her last album.

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:53.080
But Meg wasn’t talking about the singer
Poe, she was referencing our boy Edgar Allen.

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:57.170
More specifically, she’s probably talking
about his short horror story, The Premature

00:02:57.170 --> 00:03:00.730
Burial, which is about a man with catalepsy,
a condition that can cause you to fall into

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:04.590
a trance where you lose your consciousness and
your body goes rigid, giving the appearance

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:05.740
of a corpse.

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:08.510
Because of this, the man becomes paranoid
of being buried alive.

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:12.819
His fear stems from claustrophobia, which
is exactly what makes Meg uncomfortable about

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:13.620
the panic room.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:17.060
When the two Altman girls are trapped in the
room later, the daughter Sarah tries to calm

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:19.570
her by saying that people never get buried
alive anymore.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:22.260
“I guess it used to happen all the time.”

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:26.510
Sarah has a condition of her own, which is
also set up in the early going, when we see

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:30.560
her medication in fridge, her Mom limiting
how much soda is poured in her glass, and

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:32.740
the blood-sugar monitor watch that she wears
to bed,

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:35.760
The camerawork in this movie is nothing short
of ridiculous.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.040
It not only does a great job of establishing
the layout of the house and showing us just

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:44.880
how close Meg and Sarah are to the intruders
when they break in, but it also has this perfect,

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:49.250
machine-like flow that represents the burglars’
methodical approach to breaking and entering.

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:52.770
We also see the camera passing through
objects and passing through walls, but for

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:56.680
the burglars, passing through solid matter
is the one thing they’d like to do that

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:57.680
they can't.

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:01.560
In my episode of Things You Missed episode on Se7en,
I explained how the main conflict in the movie

00:04:01.569 --> 00:04:05.569
is a lot like a chess match, and according
to cinematographer Conrad Hall, a similar

00:04:05.569 --> 00:04:07.520
analogy exists in Panic Room.

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:13.860
“This was always I think for him, a chess
game, and he wanted the audience to be completely

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:19.879
involved in seeing everything and knowing
everything, in many cases before the characters

00:04:19.879 --> 00:04:20.999
know them in the movie.

00:04:20.999 --> 00:04:24.749
The audience knows exactly where everybody
is in the chess game in that moment.”

00:04:24.749 --> 00:04:29.190
In addition to the camerawork, the film editing
is also used in very creative ways.

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:32.210
I love this part where cutting away is used
to censor profanity.

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:33.569
“She’s lying.”

00:04:33.569 --> 00:04:34.820
“You’re full of sh--”

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:39.500
And when Meg eventually taps into the phone
line to try to call for help, the movie is

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:42.600
cut so that we only hear what her ex-husband
Stephen hears.

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:44.280
"There are three men--

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:46.180
--stairs, they broke into the--"

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:54.040
Stephen’s new girlfriend, who is the first
to pick up the phone when Meg calls, is voiced

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:58.270
by actress Nicole Kidman, who was previously
cast to play Meg, but had to drop out due

00:04:58.270 --> 00:04:59.270
to a knee injury.

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:03.259
They actually shot for three weeks with Nicole
before Jodie Foster had to be brought in to

00:05:03.259 --> 00:05:04.259
replace her.

00:05:04.259 --> 00:05:08.960
So Jodie replaced Nicole in the movie, but
Nicole replaced Jodie in the marriage (in

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:09.960
the movie).

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:13.339
But there would be another interesting cameo
in Panic Room, when we’re introduced to

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:17.900
the neighbor across the way who is the key
to finding even more Things You Missed.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:33.540
In a moment of desperation, Sarah eventually discovers
a ventilation hole to the outside, and uses

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:36.169
a flashlight to try to send a message to their
neighbor.

00:05:36.169 --> 00:05:40.319
The neighbor, who is credited as “Sleepy
Neighbor” is played by Andrew Kevin Walker,

00:05:40.319 --> 00:05:42.780
who was the screenwriter of Se7en.

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:47.249
That means the three central characters of
Se7en, Detective Somerset, Detective Mills

00:05:47.249 --> 00:05:49.210
and John Doe are his creations.

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:53.180
I think these three all have spiritual successors
in Panic Room.

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:56.819
Forest Whitaker plays Burnham, a smart calm,
logical voice of reason.

00:05:56.819 --> 00:06:00.370
He’s only in on the heist because he wants
a better life for his kid, similar to how

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:04.900
Detective Somerset may have used illegal tactics
to help locate John Doe, but it came from

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:07.839
a good place, because he was fighting for
a better world.

00:06:07.839 --> 00:06:12.529
Cornrow Leto plays Junior, the young, inexperienced
Burglar with a short temper, who gets in way

00:06:12.529 --> 00:06:13.569
over his head.

00:06:13.569 --> 00:06:17.219
He brought Burnham and Raoul together for
the heist, so he’s technically in charge,

00:06:17.219 --> 00:06:21.210
but like Detective Mills in Se7en, he doesn’t
quite know what he’s gotten himself into

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:23.219
and defaults to the others for help.

00:06:23.219 --> 00:06:25.349
Then there’s Dwight Yoakam as Raoul.

00:06:25.349 --> 00:06:28.259
He’s the crazy one, but he rarely loses
his temper.

00:06:28.259 --> 00:06:32.629
He’s always cool and calculating, even as
he negotiates a higher cut of the money they’re

00:06:32.629 --> 00:06:33.629
planning to steal.

00:06:33.629 --> 00:06:37.440
His entire personality and demeanor is very
similar to John Doe.

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:38.440
“Who are you?”

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:40.480
“I’m Raoul.”

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:44.660
"Doesn't matter who I am. Who I am means absolutely nothing."

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:49.240
So here are my spiritual succession characters,
but I should also note that you COULD make

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:54.009
a case for switching Junior and Raoul, because
(and this is a spoiler alert for Se7en, so

00:06:54.009 --> 00:06:57.710
skip ahead if you haven’t seen it) Raoul
is the one who takes out Junior, similar to

00:06:57.710 --> 00:07:02.139
what Mills eventually does to John Doe, and
Raoul’s left hand is injured when he accidentally

00:07:02.139 --> 00:07:05.919
pinches it in the panic room door, just as
Mills hurts his hand in the chase with John

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:06.480
Doe.

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:10.460
So in that case you could say that Junior
is John Doe because it was all his plan to

00:07:10.469 --> 00:07:14.220
bring everyone together like John brought
everyone together at the end of Se7en, and

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:18.339
Raoul is Mills because he lets his rage get
the better of him and he beats up Stephen,

00:07:18.339 --> 00:07:20.370
who shows up to try to save the girls.

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:24.629
And it’s also possible none of this was intentional
at all, but there’s no doubt that David

00:07:24.629 --> 00:07:29.449
Fincher’s directorial trademarks are responsible
for some similarities between these films,

00:07:29.449 --> 00:07:32.189
like the fact that it’s raining for the
majority of both movies.

00:07:32.189 --> 00:07:34.460
But he wasn’t the only one to leave his
mark on this film.

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:37.060
That’s right, I’m talking about SONY.

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:39.080
Let’s go back to the VERY beginning.

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:40.119
Columbia Pictures.

00:07:40.119 --> 00:07:42.550
A SONY Pictures Entertainment Company.

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:43.710
In the panic room.

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:44.880
SONY monitors.

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:45.880
Next to the monitors.

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:47.330
A SONY telephone.

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:48.499
The entire house.

00:07:48.499 --> 00:07:49.999
Covered by SONY cameras.

00:07:49.999 --> 00:07:54.179
I’m just surprised they didn’t give Sarah
a pair of Spyro pajamas and have her recite

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:58.180
Michael Jackson albums when she gets nervous,
the product placement in this movie is just...

00:07:58.460 --> 00:07:59.680
out of control.

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:01.540
What?

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:05.440
The big question that always comes up when
you talk about Panic Room is the ending.

00:08:05.449 --> 00:08:09.069
Burnham goes back to save the family from
Raoul and the police show up to arrest him

00:08:09.069 --> 00:08:10.369
before he can get away.

00:08:10.369 --> 00:08:14.729
As he puts his hands up, $22M swirls away
and is lost in the wind.

00:08:14.729 --> 00:08:19.139
But the question everyone seems to ask, is
how the police knew to come back after this

00:08:19.139 --> 00:08:22.710
scene, which took place earlier that night
when the cops showed up after receiving calls

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:24.990
from Meg, Stephen and a neighbor.

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:28.679
Raoul and Burnham are with Sarah in the panic
room, and they make it clear that something

00:08:28.679 --> 00:08:31.860
bad will happen to her if she tries anything
suspicious with the cops.

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:35.140
She lets them know she’s gonna try to send
them away in exchange for her daughter’s

00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:36.149
safety right here.

00:08:36.149 --> 00:08:39.210
She also knows that the thieves are watching her
every move on the video cameras.

00:08:39.210 --> 00:08:42.310
The cameras aren’t equipped with audio,
but she also knows there are two of them in

00:08:42.310 --> 00:08:45.630
there, so one of them could sneak down to
listen in on the conversation.

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:49.370
So as far as we can tell during that scene,
she sends the cops away without raising the

00:08:49.370 --> 00:08:52.790
alarm, and Burnham and Raoul still think they
have a chance to get away with it.

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:57.149
However, if we take a closer look at the scene,
we can see Meg is very deliberate about this

00:08:57.149 --> 00:08:58.780
interaction with the police.

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:02.440
Earlier on, when she was able to temporarily
hook up the phone line, she made two calls.

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:05.940
The first was to the police, who put her on
hold, so she promptly hangs up and makes the

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:07.610
second call to her ex-husband.

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:11.250
Remember, she was in the panic room when she
made these calls, so the bad guys didn’t

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:14.810
hear either of them, and she’s able to use
that to her advantage by giving subtle clues

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:17.420
to the cops that something doesn’t add up.

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:18.420
Roll it.

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:21.899
“We got a call ma’am.”

00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:23.940
“Somebody called you?”

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:25.959
This is the first sign that something is up.

00:09:25.959 --> 00:09:30.130
She was the first one to make a call, and
now she’s acting like it never happened.

00:09:30.130 --> 00:09:31.380
Then look at this next part.

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:32.960
“Can we come in?”

00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:34.760
“What do you want?”

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:36.480
“We’d like to come in.”

00:09:36.490 --> 00:09:38.460
“No, you can’t come in.”

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:39.720
Pause it right there.

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:44.170
As she steps back she leans against the second
door, opening it ever so slightly so that

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:48.240
the cop can see the couch pushed up against
the door, and possibly the man bleeding out

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:49.310
in the living room.

00:09:49.310 --> 00:09:53.120
Then she turns away to close the inner door,
calling even more attention to it.

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:57.310
But perhaps the most important part of the
conversation is this.

00:09:58.060 --> 00:09:59.660
“There were three.”

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:01.800
“What?”

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:08.020
“Your husband said you called for help,
and you said, there were three right before

00:10:08.029 --> 00:10:09.300
you got cut off?

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:12.379
What was the end of that sentence?”

00:10:12.379 --> 00:10:16.232
So Meg makes up a story about how she drunkenly
called her ex-husband and said there are three

00:10:16.232 --> 00:10:19.541
things that she would do if he drove over
and hopped in bed with her right now, but

00:10:19.541 --> 00:10:22.100
she came to her senses and hung up mid-sentence.

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:26.700
But if we go back even earlier in the night
to examine that phone call, she says,

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:29.480
“Stephen, you gotta help us.

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:31.340
There are three men…”

00:10:31.350 --> 00:10:35.740
So this is the perfect lie to tell them, because
she knows it’s wrong, the cops know it’s

00:10:35.740 --> 00:10:39.420
wrong, and the robbers know it’s wrong,
but robbers don’t know that the cops know

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:40.120
it’s wrong.

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:42.220
They think she’s making up a good excuse.

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:44.100
And then there’s one final test.

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:45.100
“Ma’am.

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:51.520
If there’s something you want to say to
us, but maybe you can’t say it right now.

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:53.680
You might want to make some kind of a signal.

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:57.760
Like blinking your eyes a few times.

00:10:57.769 --> 00:10:59.670
Something like that.”

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:04.160
And we never see Meg blink her eyes, but the
scene cuts back to the close-up of the officer,

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:07.509
so it’s left unknown for the rest of the
movie if she blinked at him while she was

00:11:07.509 --> 00:11:08.509
off-screen.

00:11:08.509 --> 00:11:11.779
But after seeing the ending, I think she did
give a signal, and the cop was smart enough

00:11:11.779 --> 00:11:15.199
to put all those pieces together and know
that it wasn’t the right time for them to

00:11:15.199 --> 00:11:16.480
storm into the house.

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:20.870
But perhaps an even bigger mystery is why
there isn’t a Blu-Ray available for this

00:11:20.870 --> 00:11:21.870
movie.

00:11:21.870 --> 00:11:24.690
I mean Sony has already demonstrated that
they want to promote the heck out of themselves

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:28.709
by drenching this movie in product placement,
so it seems like a no brainer to put this

00:11:28.709 --> 00:11:33.839
beloved film on Blu-Ray, it’ll probably
have ads for Sony movies inside the box, trailers

00:11:33.839 --> 00:11:35.310
-- it’s a win-win for everyone.

00:11:35.310 --> 00:11:37.870
So you know what, internet, let’s make this
happen already.

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:40.410
I’m gonna put a link in the description,
let’s get this thing going.

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:44.290
And hey, if you love horror, you don’t need
to panic, because you’re in the right place.

00:11:44.290 --> 00:11:48.339
I’m gonna be covering all of David Fincher’s
horror-thriller type movies, most of them

00:11:48.339 --> 00:11:52.569
are probably more thriller-mystery but that’s
OK, if there’s suspense or creepiness, like

00:11:52.569 --> 00:11:55.621
in this case Jared Leto’s haircut, I’m
game to cover it, and you’ll find it in

00:11:55.621 --> 00:11:57.040
that playlist on the left.

00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:01.820
Remember to subscribe to CZsWorld for new
horrors every week, ring that deathbell for

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:07.360
notifications and I’ll see you in the next
one, assuming we stay inside.

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:08.900
Of the panic room.

